# Capability grants for a local agent — EXAMPLE, NOT ACTIVE.
#
# The agent reads ~/.community-member/grants.policy. This file is not that
# file and is never loaded. Copy it, delete everything you do not need, and
# uncomment only what this install is for:
#
#     cp grants.policy.example ~/.community-member/grants.policy
#
# With no grants.policy, every file, shell, network, browser and desktop action
# is refused. That is the default and it is a working state: the agent still
# serves its card, answers signed requests, drains its inbox and runs its
# deterministic skills. Grant only what this install needs to do.
#
# Every line below is commented out. A grants file is not a checklist to
# complete — an install that needs none of these should have none of them.
#
# The agent reports a refused action with the exact grant line that would have
# permitted it, so the shortest way to build this file is to run the agent and
# add lines as it tells you what it wanted.

# ── How arguments are bounded, per capability ─────────────────────────
#
#   fs.*              path globs. ~ expands, ** spans directories.
#   net.http          hostnames. *.example.com needs at least one label.
#   browser.*         ORIGINS. Scheme required and exact; port defaults
#                     from the scheme.
#   shell.exec        BINARY NAMES ONLY. Read the next section.
#   desktop.*         target names.

# ── The one thing to read before granting a shell binary ──────────────
#
# shell.exec says WHICH BINARY may run. The agent narrows one shape of
# argument — one naming an existing file outside your fs.* grants — and
# nothing else. A binary that takes a URL, or a path written inside a program
# it interprets, is not bounded by the patterns you wrote elsewhere:
#
#     fs.read:~/Downloads/**      <- looks like "only Downloads"
#     shell.exec:awk              <- reads any file, if the path is in the program
#
#   awk '{print}' /etc/shadow            names the path as an argument: refused.
#   awk 'BEGIN{getline l < "/etc/shadow"}'   puts it inside the program: runs.
#
# Same binary, same grants, opposite outcomes. The narrowing reads arguments,
# not what a binary does with them. The same escape exists for curl and wget
# (a host is not a path), for tee and cp writing files that do not exist yet,
# and for sh, bash, python, node, env, xargs and find, which run other binaries
# and so subsume the binary allowlist itself.
#
# The agent prints a warning at startup naming any such grant. Prefer a binary
# that does one job. If you need a general one, grant it deliberately and know
# that it is the widest line in your file.

# ── Files ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Read a directory you drop work into. Prefer the narrowest path that works.
# fs.read:~/Downloads/agent/**

# Write only where the agent's own output belongs — never a source tree, a
# config directory, or anywhere you would not want a file replaced.
# fs.write:~/Documents/agent-output/*.md

# ── Network ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# One hostname per service the agent actually calls. A leading *. covers
# subdomains and not the parent, so *.example.com does not include
# example.com.
# net.http:api.example.com

# ── Browser ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Origins, with the scheme. Browser navigation uses a persistent profile that
# holds the agent's cookies, so granting http:// puts them on the wire in
# clear. Grant https:// unless you have a reason not to.
# browser.navigate:https://docs.example.com
# browser.navigate:https://*.wikipedia.org

# ── Shell ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# This section suggests nothing, and that is not an oversight.
#
# The narrowing above reaches one shape of argument and nothing else, so
# there is still no binary that can be recommended as the narrow choice. git looks like one — it is about
# repositories, not about arbitrary paths — and it is not: a config alias,
# core.pager, core.sshCommand or a repository hook each run any command you
# like. The same is true of make, tar, docker and every editor with a shell
# escape. Any suggestion here would be read as a vetted default, and there is
# no default that is safe to hand out.
#
# The agent warns about the binaries it knows subsume another capability, and
# it does NOT know them all. A grant it says nothing about has not been
# assessed. Every shell.exec line is the widest line in your policy.
#
# So: write one only if you know what that binary can be made to do with
# arguments you did not choose, and prefer solving the problem without a shell
# grant at all.
#
# shell.exec:<binary>
